WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
Quantum mechanics is the most successful theory for describing nature,

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.300
but how does it actually work?

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.960
Imagine you're in a world where things aren't what they seem,

4
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:16.460
where particles behave like waves and at the same time like solid objects.

5
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.840
This is the world of quantum mechanics, a mystery that challenges us

6
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.800
to rethink our understanding of reality.

7
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.900
This year quantum mechanics marks one hundred years of existence,

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.420
which for science is a remarkable anniversary.

9
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.680
In that time it has become the most accurate theory

10
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.340
we have for predicting natural phenomena.

11
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.640
For example, thanks to quantum mechanics

12
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.000
we can determine exactly how electrons will move around an atom

13
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.320
or how light behaves as it passes through different materials.

14
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.680
Nevertheless, despite its success, a key question arises:

15
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.080
how does it work and what is really going on at the microscopic level.

16
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.620
Imagine you're looking at the world from an insect's point of view.

17
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.560
Things seem much more complicated than they look from a human point of view.

18
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.680
At the micro level, particles move unpredictably, as if playing hide-and-seek.

19
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.840
This uncertainty is what makes quantum mechanics so intriguing, but confusing.

20
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:27.060
As we approach these concepts we will uncover layers of mystery that shape our universe.

21
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.580
Now we'll return to the basic concepts of quantum mechanics.

22
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.960
Get ready for a journey through a world in which the boundaries between reality and illusion blur.

23
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.480
When we talk about the word "quantum," what does it actually mean?

24
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.960
Essentially, "quantum" means discrete, portioned units of energy.

25
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.140
Imagine energy is a continuously flowing river.

26
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.980
In classical mechanics this river is considered smooth and continuous.

27
00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.360
However, in the world of quantum mechanics this river turns into a hundred little waterfalls

28
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.380
falling off the edges of a cliff.

29
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.180
Each drop represents a certain unit of energy, which can be considered a quantum.

30
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.180
This discretization is key to understanding how the microworld works.

31
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.380
Quantum mechanics deals with predicting the behavior of particles based on known factors,

32
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.840
but this is where things get interesting.

33
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:31.180
While classical mechanics calculates the trajectory of particles as if they move along well-defined paths,

34
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:37.580
quantum mechanics teaches us that particles often behave like waves rather than solid bodies.

35
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.500
Think of it as a dance of light spreading through space,

36
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.620
where the trajectory can never be determined exactly.

37
00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:51.220
This is the moment when the boundaries between reality and illusion begin to blur.

38
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.420
Now that we've dived a little deeper into the basic principles of quantum mechanics,

39
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.100
the question arises of how all this applies to atoms.

40
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.840
In the next step we'll explore how quantum mechanics

41
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.660
shapes our perception of atoms and their interactions.

42
00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.400
It will be a journey full of surprises and discoveries.

43
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.440
How do atoms work and why don't they behave like classical particles?

44
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.700
This question leads us to the very heart of quantum mechanics

45
00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.860
and reveals the fascinating sides of the world of microscopic particles.

46
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.480
When talking about atoms, we usually imagine a nucleus surrounded by electrons

47
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.640
that move around it like planets around the Sun.

48
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.420
However, reality is much more complicated.

49
00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.120
Atoms consist of a nucleus formed by protons and neutrons,

50
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:45.080
and electrons, instead of moving along precisely defined trajectories, behave like waves.

51
00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:51.800
This wave nature of electrons means we can't predict exactly where they will be at any moment.

52
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:57.760
Instead we can only talk about the probability of their presence in certain places.

53
00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.720
The phenomenon of quantum tunneling complicates our understanding of these particles even more.

54
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.540
Imagine there's a wall in front of you and you want to get to the other side.

55
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.040
In the classical world you'd have to physically pass through the wall.

56
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.340
However, in the quantum world an electron can somehow pass through the wall as if it weren't there.

57
00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:21.580
This phenomenon challenges our intuition and raises questions about the limits of the possible.

58
00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:26.260
Now that we've delved into the structure of the atom and the phenomena that define it,

59
00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:33.260
it's time to focus on quantum properties and find out how they affect our understanding of reality.

60
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.980
How do you see this unusual nature of atoms?

61
00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.100
Do you think it's possible that what we consider real may actually be an illusion?

62
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.260
Share your thoughts in the comments, because sometimes the best answers come from your point of view.

63
00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.260
Quantum mechanics often seems like a puzzle that keeps slipping away from our understanding.

64
00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.760
Why is quantum mechanics so unpredictable?

65
00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.420
Imagine you're trying to catch the wind with a net.

66
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.880
The more you try, the more it seems to slip out of your hands.

67
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.640
This metaphor perfectly reflects the nature of the quantum world,

68
00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:11.740
where things seem much more complicated than they look.

69
00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:17.560
One of the key properties of quantum objects is that they can't simultaneously have

70
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.620
precisely defined values of speed and position.

71
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:27.080
This is called Heisenberg's uncertainty principle and it represents a fundamental property of the quantum world.

72
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:33.700
When we try to measure an electron's position, for example, we lose accuracy in determining its speed.

73
00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.480
This phenomenon poses a question for us.

74
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:41.420
How can we know anything about something that is so inherently uncertain?

75
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.800
Randomness is another fascinating feature of the quantum world.

76
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.400
Quantum mechanics predicts not exact events, but only probabilities.

77
00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.960
This means that instead of saying that a certain event will definitely happen,

78
00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.500
we can say that there is a certain percentage probability that it will happen.

79
00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.080
This idea can be confusing, but it also calls on us

80
00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.780
to rethink our perception of control and predictability.

81
00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.900
Now that we've explored quantum uncertainty and randomness,

82
00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.480
it's time to turn to the concept of superposition and its meaning.

83
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.080
How do you interpret this idea of layered possibilities that coexist?

84
00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:31.900
Share your thoughts in the comments, because your point of view may open new horizons in understanding these abstract concepts.

85
00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.100
Superposition is one of the most fascinating concepts in quantum mechanics,

86
00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.260
one that forces us to rethink everything we know about the nature of reality.

87
00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:46.020
When we talk about superposition, the question arises of what it actually means.

88
00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.500
Imagine a particle that is in a state that is neither one nor the other,

89
00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.360
but encompasses several possibilities at once.

90
00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.640
For example, when we think about electrons, they don't exist in a single state,

91
00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.200
but in a state of superposition, where they can be in many places at once.

92
00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:08.340
This idea may seem absurd, but that is precisely the essence of quantum mechanics.

93
00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.780
Quantum states are like the complex harmonies of music,

94
00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:16.520
where different tones intertwine to create a single melody.

95
00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.880
When a measurement is made, the system collapses into one of the possible states,

96
00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:27.320
as if you pressed a button that selects only one note from this rich symphony.

97
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:33.240
Think about how in everyday life we often choose between different options.

98
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.860
Superposition shows us that at the quantum level even decisions aren't so simple.

99
00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:45.280
Particles use their ability to be in different states until the moment we observe them.

100
00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:55.780
This process calls our perception of reality into question and prompts us to think that everything we see is just one part of a broader spectrum of possibilities.

101
00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:01.960
Now that we've delved into the phenomenon of superposition and its role in quantum systems,

102
00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:06.280
it's time to turn to the role of the observer in quantum mechanics.

103
00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.480
How do you see the interaction between the observer and the quantum system?

104
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:16.200
Share your thoughts in the comments, because your point of view may open new horizons

105
00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.000
in understanding these abstract concepts.

106
00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:23.440
The role of the observer in quantum mechanics is a topic that opens the door

107
00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.020
to many philosophical and scientific debates.

108
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.940
How do observers affect quantum systems?

109
00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:35.960
This question leads us to understand how the mere presence of an observer can change the state of a quantum system.

110
00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.860
Imagine a situation as if you're watching a movie.

111
00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:46.780
Every time you press the pause button, the action stops, but doesn't change until you decide to continue watching.

112
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:55.880
In the quantum world, observation acts like this pause button, where the very act of observation changes the dynamics of what's happening.

113
00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:02.340
Quantum phenomena are elusive precisely because they can't be observed without affecting them.

114
00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:07.200
For example, when we observe an electron, its state changes.

115
00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.440
It's no longer just a wave, but becomes a particle.

116
00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:14.560
This interaction between the observer and the object of study

117
00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:18.400
raises fundamental questions about the nature of reality and knowledge.

118
00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.240
Does reality exist independently of our observation,

119
00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.280
or is our understanding merely a reflection of our own experience?

120
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.100
Now that we've dived into this complex topic,

121
00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.480
it's time to sum up the key points

122
00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.360
and move on to discussing the implications of quantum mechanics.

123
00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.600
What does all this mean for our understanding of the world around us?

124
00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.100
What consequences does it carry in light of modern science and philosophy?

125
00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.340
And while you reflect on these questions,

126
00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.540
I urge you to share your thoughts in the comments.

127
00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.860
Your point of view may open new horizons in our understanding.

128
00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.480
How does the wave function collapse during measurement?

129
00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.740
This question, which at first glance seems simple,

130
00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:08.060
actually opens the door to one of the most intriguing mysteries of quantum mechanics.

131
00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:13.120
Essentially, the wave function is a mathematical description of a quantum system,

132
00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:19.520
and the collapse of this function occurs when we make a measurement and get a specific result.

133
00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.980
But what really happens at that moment?

134
00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:30.580
To better understand this complex phenomenon, we need to turn to the postulates of quantum mechanics and their application.

135
00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:39.500
The first postulate states that a quantum system can be described by a wave function containing all possible information about that system.

136
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.860
However, when we obtain one result with a measuring instrument, the wave function collapses into that result.

137
00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:54.260
A picture you can imagine: we have many possibilities in a sort of magic box.

138
00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:59.360
All the options are in there, but only when we open the box do we find just one.

139
00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.780
Of course, this leads us to many ambiguities.

140
00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.000
When exactly does the collapse occur?

141
00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:10.340
Does it happen the moment the measurement begins, or only when we observe it?

142
00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:14.980
The question of cause also arises. What really triggers this collapse?

143
00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:19.320
In this game of the quantum world, the measuring instrument plays a key role.

144
00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:24.100
It isn't just a passive observer, it actively influences the system it measures.

145
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:28.400
Imagine a situation with electrons moving through a double slit.

146
00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:34.240
When we don't observe them, they behave like waves, passing through both slits at once.

147
00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:40.820
However, when we place a detector to measure them, they immediately choose one path, collapsing into particles.

148
00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:49.660
This interaction between the measuring instrument and the quantum system opens new questions about the nature of reality and the participants of this experiment.

149
00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:55.600
As we see, the problems of wave function collapse bring us to the limits of our understanding.

150
00:11:56.020 --> 00:12:00.520
Many physicists have tried to resolve this mystery by proposing different theories.

151
00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.820
For example, some explanations suggest that collapse is the result of an objective reality

152
00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:10.480
arising from the interaction between the observer and the system.

153
00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:20.660
Another theory, known as decoherence, speaks about how quantum systems lose their coherent properties when exposed to the external environment.

154
00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:29.900
Now that we've clarified some key aspects of wave function collapse, it's time to move on to the phenomenon of entanglement in quantum mechanics.

155
00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:35.400
How does this intriguing concept complicate our understanding of the quantum world even further?

156
00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:41.880
Thanks to entanglement, quantum objects end up connected in a way that surpasses our everyday experience.

157
00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:46.800
And, as we'll soon see, this connectedness may have profound implications

158
00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.240
for our perception of reality and the way we understand the universe.

159
00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:56.720
The phenomenon of entanglement is one of the most intriguing aspects of quantum mechanics.

160
00:12:57.260 --> 00:13:03.560
When we talk about entanglement, we're asking what it really means for quantum systems to be entangled.

161
00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:08.720
Essentially, entanglement refers to a state in which quantum objects,

162
00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:13.940
such as electrons or photons, are connected so that a change in the state of one

163
00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:18.480
immediately affects the state of the other, regardless of the distance between them.

164
00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:22.460
This connection works like a secret thread that binds them,

165
00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:28.380
even when they are far from each other, which raises deep questions about the nature of reality.

166
00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:31.860
The definition of entanglement may seem simple,

167
00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.760
but its implications are profound and complex.

168
00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:39.220
For example, imagine you have two quantum particles that are entangled.

169
00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:43.240
When you measure the state of one, you immediately know the state of the other,

170
00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.240
no matter how far away it is.

171
00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:50.760
This leads to a surprising phenomenon, known as instantaneous action,

172
00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:53.640
where information travels faster than light,

173
00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:57.040
which violates our classical understanding of causality.

174
00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:04.740
This concept provokes philosophical reflection on what it means to know something in the quantum world.

175
00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:09.520
Experiments with entangled states give additional insight into this topic.

176
00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:18.440
For example, the famous experiment by Alain Aspect shows how entanglement works even when the particles are far apart.

177
00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:24.380
In this experiment, researchers used laser beams to produce entangled photons.

178
00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:29.360
When they measured the state of one photon, the results immediately revealed the state of the other,

179
00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:32.060
no matter how far apart they were physically separated.

180
00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:36.280
These experiments not only confirm the existence of entanglement,

181
00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:41.380
but also trigger a fundamental rethinking of our ideas about space and time.

182
00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:45.040
How does entanglement affect measurements and results?

183
00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:49.260
Well, when we decide to measure one of the entangled systems,

184
00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.380
it automatically changes the state of the other system.

185
00:14:52.380 --> 00:15:03.420
This interaction can be compared to a dance where every movement of one dancer inevitably affects the movements of the other, creating unpredictable and complex patterns.

186
00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:09.580
This dependence between the systems is what makes entanglement so fascinating and bewildering.

187
00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:15.020
Next we'll look at how entanglement shows up in everyday life.

188
00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:21.800
How can this unusual connection between quantum objects affect our perception and experience?

189
00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:27.520
Is it possible that some aspects of our existence are based on these quantum connections?

190
00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:31.140
Stay with us as we explore this intriguing topic

191
00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:34.780
and find out how present entanglement is in our lives.

192
00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:39.900
Before we continue: if you're enjoying this journey through the world of quantum mechanics,

193
00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:45.760
don't forget to subscribe to the channel and like the video — right now, before you forget.

194
00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:50.280
You can always unsubscribe if you don't like it, but trust us.

195
00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:52.960
You won't want to miss what comes next.

196
00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.220
The many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics is a fascinating idea

197
00:15:57.220 --> 00:16:01.480
that helps us understand complex phenomena at the microscopic level.

198
00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.480
How does this interpretation explain quantum phenomena?

199
00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:10.600
Imagine a world in which your every decision creates a new reality,

200
00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.760
where every possibility is realized in different universes.

201
00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:19.420
In this interpretation, when we encounter a quantum event,

202
00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:24.020
such as measuring the position of an electron, there isn't just one reality.

203
00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:30.480
Instead there is a countless multitude of parallel worlds in which each of the possibilities plays out.

204
00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:36.920
The core ideas of the many-worlds interpretation, popularized by physicist Hugh Everett III,

205
00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:44.300
rely on the concept of superposition — a state in which particles can exist in several states at once.

206
00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:51.560
When we measure the state of a particle, our observations don't affect what happens inside these other realities.

207
00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:58.920
In each of these worlds the electron can be in different places depending on all possible measurement outcomes.

208
00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:03.040
This leads us to the conclusion that all these worlds are equally real,

209
00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.000
but separated from each other like pages in a book that has never been opened.

210
00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:11.080
Imagine you're standing at a crossroads.

211
00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:14.860
Each path represents a different decision you can make.

212
00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:20.380
In one universe you might decide to turn left, and in another you'll turn right.

213
00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:24.840
Each decision opens new doors, new experiences and new destinies.

214
00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:30.700
This philosophical dimension of the many-worlds interpretation brings us to questions of ethics.

215
00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:36.180
If all our choices are real at the same time, how does that affect our responsibility?

216
00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:43.340
Are we really responsible for everything we do, if we know that in some other universe things happen differently?

217
00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:49.460
Now, reflecting on these ethical questions, we come to yet another layer of complexity.

218
00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:54.120
How do our actions in this universe intertwine with actions in others?

219
00:17:54.520 --> 00:18:00.200
Do our deeds have consequences only here, or also in alternative realities?

220
00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:07.260
Questions like these prompt us to rethink the limits of our understanding of morality and responsibility.

221
00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:15.120
By exploring this many-worlds interpretation, we can wonder how it shapes our everyday life.

222
00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.140
Are we aware of all the possibilities offered to us,

223
00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:24.760
how would we behave if we knew that each of our decisions has its own universe?

224
00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.520
These reflections open the door to deep philosophical discussions

225
00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:34.300
and allow us to see our place within the broader framework of the cosmos.

226
00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:39.460
Now we'll turn to alternative interpretations of quantum mechanics.

227
00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:44.700
What do they all offer and how do they differ from the many-worlds interpretation?

228
00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:50.820
Stay with us as we explore this mysterious topic and open new horizons.

229
00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.860
What other interpretations are there besides many-worlds?

230
00:18:54.860 --> 00:19:00.820
In the world of quantum mechanics, where the rules of particle behavior seem almost magical,

231
00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:06.300
there are different ways to understand what is really going on at the microscopic level.

232
00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:10.540
The Copenhagen interpretation, which is perhaps the best known,

233
00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:14.620
offers a fascinating perspective, but is not without criticism.

234
00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:19.540
The Copenhagen interpretation, developed by Niels Bohr and Werner Heisenberg,

235
00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:24.920
holds that the world of quantum particles cannot be understood as a classical world.

236
00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:30.960
Instead it emphasizes that the act of observation itself affects the observed system.

237
00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:34.920
This idea may sound like something from a science fiction movie,

238
00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:38.840
but it is deeply rooted in experimental results.

239
00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:43.140
For example, when we observe an electron, its position or speed

240
00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.380
depends on the way we measure it.

241
00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:48.700
This leads to the famous Heisenberg uncertainty,

242
00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:53.480
which says that we cannot simultaneously know the exact place and speed of a particle.

243
00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:58.560
Critics of this interpretation often point to its inherent subjectivity:

244
00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.900
how can something exist only when we look at it?

245
00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:05.760
Does that mean reality itself depends on us?

246
00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:08.600
On the other hand, there's the pilot wave.

247
00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:15.100
This theory, proposed by Louis de Broglie, suggests that particles like electrons are not only particles,

248
00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.920
but also waves that guide them through space.

249
00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:24.320
The pilot wave is a metaphor that helps imagine how particles can have wave properties,

250
00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:28.860
allowing them to behave like conductors in the quantum world.

251
00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:32.900
This approach tries to explain the phenomenon of superposition,

252
00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:36.040
when a particle can be in several states at once.

253
00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:41.360
Imagine a flowing river. Particles move along different paths,

254
00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:43.040
but they are all parts of the same water.

255
00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:48.960
Critics, however, point out that the pilot wave doesn't explain all aspects of quantum behavior,

256
00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:52.460
especially when it comes to interactions between particles.

257
00:20:53.080 --> 00:21:01.180
Then we have quantum realism, which focuses on the idea that quantum objects possess their own reality independent of the observer.

258
00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.340
This approach introduces an interesting dilemma.

259
00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:11.380
If quantum particles have a property that exists without our observation, what does that say about the nature of reality?

260
00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:16.760
This philosophical discussion opens the door to questions about the subjectivity of measurement.

261
00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:23.260
How our measurements shape the reality around us while still remaining true to quantum laws.

262
00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.500
Finally we'll look at practical applications of quantum mechanics.

263
00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:35.400
Reflecting on these interpretations, it's important to understand how they affect the technologies we use daily.

264
00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:40.120
From quantum computers, promising a revolution in data processing,

265
00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:45.500
to quantum communication, which can protect our information like never before,

266
00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:48.360
the possibilities are almost limitless.

267
00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:52.460
Our future depends on these fundamental principles,

268
00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:59.380
and understanding alternative interpretations will help us better grasp the world we live in.

269
00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:05.620
So stay with us as we explore how these theories shape our everyday life

270
00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:10.340
and how quantum mechanics can make the world a better place to live.

271
00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:15.380
Quantum mechanics, often perceived as a mystery unfolding at the tiniest scales,

272
00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:19.380
actually has a profound influence on our everyday life and technology.

273
00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:24.320
How does quantum mechanics affect technology and everyday life?

274
00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:30.820
These questions are not merely theoretical, they are the essence of the development of innovations that shape the future.

275
00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:40.200
Imagine quantum computers as machines that use the principles of quantum mechanics to process data faster than we can imagine today.

276
00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:48.660
While traditional computers use bits as basic units of information, quantum computers use quantum bits, or qubits.

277
00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:57.240
These qubits can be in the state "zero," "one," or in both at once thanks to phenomena such as superposition.

278
00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:04.740
This means a quantum computer can simultaneously solve problems that would take a classical computer a lifetime.

279
00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:11.380
In some cases quantum computers will be able to perform calculations that are out of reach today,

280
00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:16.200
such as simulating complex molecules or optimizing large systems.

281
00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:22.500
On the other hand, quantum cryptography represents a revolution in data security.

282
00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:26.900
The way we encrypt information today may come under threat.

283
00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:33.860
But with quantum cryptography, security can be guaranteed in a way that was impossible before.

284
00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:44.040
Using the principles of quantum entanglement, information can be transmitted in such a way that it cannot be eavesdropped on without revealing the presence of an outsider.

285
00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:49.500
It's like a safety net that switches on automatically when someone tries to approach,

286
00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:51.880
signaling that something has gone wrong.

287
00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:55.740
So, in a world where data is increasingly under attack,

288
00:23:56.200 --> 00:24:00.160
quantum cryptography may become the key to secure communication.

289
00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:04.920
The influence of quantum mechanics on science and technology is broad and deep.

290
00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:09.780
For example, research in quantum biology tries to clarify

291
00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:12.920
how the animal world uses quantum effects.

292
00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:21.220
Bird migrations, which happen with incredible precision, may be driven by quantum processes in their sense organs.

293
00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:28.920
This opens the door to new knowledge about how nature uses quantum principles to survive and thrive.

294
00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:39.320
As we approach the end of this journey through quantum mechanics, it's important to note that this field of science not only leads us to understand the universe,

295
00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:42.720
but also to improve our everyday lives.

296
00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:46.720
Now we'll finish with conclusions about quantum mechanics.

297
00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:52.040
Understanding these principles can help us better grasp the world we live in,

298
00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:58.000
and applying this knowledge can open doors to innovations that will shape our future.

299
00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:04.200
Before we say goodbye, we want to encourage you to share your thoughts on these topics.

300
00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:09.480
Have you ever thought about how quantum computers could change your life?

301
00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:13.740
Your point of view is valuable, so feel free to leave a comment below.

302
00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:18.620
Thank you for being with us on this journey through the mysteries of quantum mechanics.